#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179493 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8033e21-86ab-5dc6-8060-a0b5c134bf9d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regions Where a Function Is Positive or Negative Using a Sign Table"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "56f7f36b-4e9c-5a86-bd20-ca01d9fce41f" :question/math-academy-id "e-9412" :question/problem "Consider the expression $f(x,y) = (3-x)(y+1)^2.$  What are the missing entries in the sign table of $f(x,y)$ shown below?\n\n| $y > -1$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y <               -1$ |  |  |\n|  | $x<   3$ | $x> 3$ |" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x = 3$ and $y = -1.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/df8519853487a7da738b4b3c477b141e00287c67dcb9eb804cbd385c6f43b611.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/4666739c474f85cbf8de7e7ca183206ab7b0bd804816a5504f9c1d97b03956b4.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $(3-x)$ | $(y+1)^2$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-left | $(2,0)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(4,0)$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(2,-2)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(4,-2)$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n\n \n\nUsing the *last* column of the table above, we get the following picture:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/e789ce41c696b456e0c9d79ba8ce60aba030e454ab614902ea13c8d9ccd02d8c.png)\n\nFinally, we fill in the required sign table.\n\n| $y > -1$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y <               -1$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n|  | $x<  3$ | $x> 3$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179494 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ef881ff-71d4-5268-9cac-32c478dc02a5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regions in the Plane Where a Function Is Positive or Negative"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "222d8f84-be89-5bcf-94f3-f309635bbcbf" :question/math-academy-id "e-9414" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y) = (x-2)^2(y+1).$ Which of the following shaded areas gives the solution to the inequality $f(x,y) > 0?$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=2$ and $y=-1.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below. Since we have a strict inequality, we use dashed lines to divide the plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/8e635d2ba2e7ca5721ff8b1dec2055b4ac0c9bcc2eca648848c27190271e92b6.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/1ce934096086de33423910710a010400922ea86c66bbfe0b1bfb0d92291ab59e.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $(x-2)^2$ | $(y+1)$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(1,0)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(3,0)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(1,-2)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(3,-2)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n\n \n\nFinally, on our diagram, we shade in the solutions to $f(x,y) > 0$ using the *last* column of the table above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/70a2ca01e47e91f9d818599886f79b0f00edad0cfd12b080995dfbe6cdf68235.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296055 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ef881ff-71d4-5268-9cac-32c478dc02a5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regions in the Plane Where a Function Is Positive or Negative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51cdf062-77d3-53fc-b3a0-4ccd9f0d2e1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/330f6080a3640f193e3d1650405030c049f059aaa9e3c6f280d2355382ed2e69.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296057} {:answer/id #uuid "109d3ab8-3738-54b4-aee0-77f5d905df8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/ff554d3264fe5aeb9cca37a143888c8eb7a7e31f9bcdb3f8512309e45397aeb2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296058} {:answer/id #uuid "f3c669d3-d855-58be-8f27-56cead888a48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/02a01c1a57b61374e7f9c44b7609f7c0b48c9f91a44ffd0d4a86afeb0909ed68.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296059} {:answer/id #uuid "3da33b3f-5b9a-5853-9477-74cbb9fcc973" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/ba2d7fbeb1a79d32f499e3c3ae81595e4a58fdd4486c6e558fc129fe0e5b3385.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296060} {:answer/id #uuid "d533bd23-d1ab-578e-aee5-b52d4c72a90c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/d1958e53fc544743a537eccab139fa33716823e3b57b4aeac90c3b17ec7e8e48.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296061}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3da33b3f-5b9a-5853-9477-74cbb9fcc973" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/ba2d7fbeb1a79d32f499e3c3ae81595e4a58fdd4486c6e558fc129fe0e5b3385.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296060} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd4a7138-7632-523f-84fa-1bf20cd8015f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296056}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cf0f09c4-74cb-5db2-aad0-f5a0e418906f" :question/math-academy-id "q-149553" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y)=x(2-y).$ Which of the following shaded areas gives the solution to the inequality $f(x,y)>0?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=0$ and $y=2.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below. Since we have a strict inequality, we use dashed lines to divide the plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/41f10e527834d0767709b965150054a564eaee9c174204381ed94b0e43077fe0.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/994e35e23e56191ef06a155912a326d28d857f236308655265d18d17bb48531b.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $x$ | $(2-y)$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(-1,3)$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(1,3)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-1,1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(1,1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\n \n\nFinally, on our diagram, we shade in the solutions to $f(x,y)>0$ using the *last* column of the table above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/9710358526e0e14fbf7263ba5d1496cdcb2941f9cb32a1d07b2ea37865163ab2.png)"}]}
